              Achilles tendons, and cardiovascular system have adapted is a
              common cause of early soreness and frustration.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Does treadmill incline burn more calories than speed?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Incline is generally the more joint-friendly way to raise
              calorie burn, since even a modest grade increases effort
              substantially without the impact forces that come with running
              faster. Many exercise physiologists consider a brisk incline
              walk comparable in calorie burn to a much faster flat jog, with
              <strong>less stress on the knees and hips</strong>.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Can incline training replace outdoor hill running?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              It gets remarkably close, especially on treadmills with true
              automatic incline and decline like the ones in this guide, but
              it isn't a perfect substitute. Treadmill inclines are precise
              and consistent, while outdoor hills add uneven terrain, wind
              resistance, and stabilizer-muscle demands that a belt can't
              fully replicate.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Is a steep incline of 30% or higher safe for daily use?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              For most healthy adults, yes, in moderation, but <strong
                >30%+ grades</strong
              > are intense enough that we'd recommend easing into them a few
              times a week rather than daily, especially early on. Anyone with
              knee, hip, or cardiovascular concerns should check with a doctor
              before regularly training at extreme inclines.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Do all incline treadmills automatically adjust the grade?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              No. Some machines require you to press up and down buttons to
              manually step the incline in increments, while others adjust
              automatically in real time to match a streamed workout's
              terrain. Automatic adjustment is more convenient for guided
              hill training but isn't required if you're comfortable
              programming your own intervals.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              How much more expensive are high-incline treadmills?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Expect a real premium once you get above roughly <strong
